    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d900">

                <pb facs="0198" n="1580" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v13p2/pg1580"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">751G.00/5–1854: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Dillon</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Department of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1954-05-18T14:00:00+01:00">May 18, 1954—2 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>4426. Sent Saigon 536 repeated information Geneva 253 Department 4426. Ranking
                    official secretariat relations Associated States informs <pb facs="0199"
                        n="1581" xml:id="pg1581"/> us that request for piastre transfers to francs
                    on part of Vietnamese officials particularly in Bao
                        Dai’s immediate entourage have grown precipitously in recent
                    days. Requests are camouflaged as “expense” (in vastly excessive amounts) for
                    negotiating delegation or, on the part of one cabinet minister, as required to
                    purchase property in France for “retirement”.</p>
                <p>Although not surprised French deplore this evidence of Vietnamese “patriots”
                    leaving sinking ship.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Dillon</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
